{
  "version": 1,
  "note": "One rule for \"someone is typing\", on both sides of the wire. `signal` is what this client reports about itself; `roster` (Round 7 B2) is what it believes about its peers. They share `rules` on purpose: the two halves are only correct in relation to each other, and splitting them into two tables is how `refreshMs > peerTtlMs` gets shipped. Times are milliseconds; every expected report carries the instant it must be made, so a rule that fires late fails as loudly as one that never fires.",
  "rules": {
    "idleStopMs": 4000,
    "refreshMs": 2500,
    "peerTtlMs": 6000,
    "invariant": "refreshMs < peerTtlMs — a client that keeps typing must re-report before the peer's belief expires, or the indicator dies mid-sentence. Assert this, do not assume it: two of the five apps deduplicated the report and never refreshed it, so a message longer than one TTL stopped showing as typing while it was still being typed.",
    "idleStopNote": "idleStopMs is the pause that ends typing, measured from the last edit, not from the last report."
  },
  "signal": {
    "note": "The composer's edits in, `report(conversationId, typing)` calls out. One conversation is typing at a time: the composer is one control, and an edit elsewhere ends the previous conversation before it starts the new one. `true` is reported at most once per refreshMs; `false` is reported the moment it is known (cleared, sent, left) and otherwise idleStopMs after the last edit. Text that trims to nothing is not typing.",
    "driver": "For each step, advance time to `at` first — firing the idle stop if its deadline falls in that span, timestamped at the deadline, not at `at` — then apply the step. After the last step, advance to `endAt`. Ops: edit{conversationId,text}, send{conversationId}, close{}, tick{}.",
