  "roster": {
    "note": "What this client believes about its peers. Facts in — someone started, someone stopped, the server listed everyone typing, a message arrived from a typer — and the people typing in a conversation out, in the order they started. Nothing here knows a wire shape: the app parses its own events and calls these, which is the boundary FR-093 drew. A belief expires `peerTtlMs` after the signal that made it, so a client that dies mid-sentence does not leave a peer typing forever.",
    "driver": "For each step, advance time to `at` first (pruning anything that expired in that span, at its own deadline), then apply the step. After the last step, advance to `endAt`. Record the watched conversation's typers whenever they change; `expect` is that whole sequence. Ops: started{conversationId,userId}, stopped{conversationId,userId}, replaced{conversationId,userIds}, sent{conversationId,senderId}, prune{}.",
    "selfId": "me",
    "cases": [
      {
        "name": "one peer types, and is forgotten a ttl after the signal",
        "watch": "c1",
        "steps": [
          {
            "at": 0,
            "op": "started",
            "conversationId": "c1",
            "userId": "u1"
          }
        ],
        "endAt": 7000,
        "expect": [
          {
            "at": 0,
            "typers": [
              "u1"
            ]
          },
          {
            "at": 6000,
            "typers": []
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "name": "a fresh signal extends the belief",
        "watch": "c1",
        "steps": [
